dia  fact checker alfonse shiondu tries to  counter online fake news he's shocked  that even politicians are promoting  rumors  one especially controversial video  features the minister of health in  neighbouring tanzania so she's  addressing on why this is very important  to for them to use alternative medicines  why we should not  trust  western medicine and stuff like that  that  was very very potentially dangerous and  very very misleading in the way it was  tied because she's saying no vaccines in  tanzania we don't have covet check this  and you will be safe from any viral  infection  in the small village of uwari too just a  few villagers are waiting under the  mango tree for a jab  harriet and her team didn't reach their  target for today  in the end they only managed 130 rather  than the planned 200 vaccinations  they plan to go out to the villages  again tomorrow  if they can get the van  sergios  is an associate professor in cellular  and molecular sciences at northumbria  university in the uk doctor a very warm  welcome  back to dw news because we spoke a year  ago actually  about covet 19 and it is still very much  here what role do you think vaccine  inequality played in the spread of this  new variant omicron  i think it's quite clear that the hub  central vaccination in developing  nations allowed  the emergence of new virus and we got  the warning shot with delta in that a  variant that was highly transmissible  basically uh was able to develop itself  in india and now um  not yet not a year on actually about  nine months on we have a new variant  that is sort of delta plus you know  omega variant which has uh much higher  apparent transvisibility than delta  and it has the capacity to  lead to an overwhelming of healthcare  systems in the developed world now if we  had  done what would what the  virology and vaccinology community had  asked which was to equitably distribute  vaccines worldwide and to sustain the  mitigation measures so that we do not  get transmission anywhere in the world  then we would be very unlikely to be  seeing this situation right now  uh unfortunately here we are we have uh  now this omicron variant um it transmits  a lot it's likely going to lead to a lot  of cases hopefully will not lead us to a  position where we will need another  lockdown  but we can't say right now because if  you got too many cases then you will  have too many people who need  hospitalization and if you don't have a  vaccine  to boost your capacity to be protected  and not become diseased then your  chances of ending up in hospital are  going to be much higher  now the world panicked and as a result  blanket travel bans were introduced as a  scientist do you think  travel bans like that are are the right  tool  right at the beginning of an outbreak  where you have a handful of people of a  disease that can be potentially  contained very quickly for example by  locking down wuhan at the time it nearly  happened  it might work  but when you have already a variant  that's transmitting in the community as  has been demonstrated in the uk and as  i'm sure the rest of europe will  demonstrate soon as the united states is  abruptly demonstrating already it's  pointless it's it's it's literally  pointless to try and prevent  reintroductions of these variant  this would work if we implemented a  lockdown in the countries where omicron  is already present and we implemented a  border control but at this point in time  when we're not prepared to lock down the  economy and communities and societies uh  the the border controls are futile  uh doctor people are very excited about  the developments of anti-virals there  are a few in the pipeline and some are  awaiting emergency approval will they be  the tool out of this pandemic  i i just want to urge a little bit of  caution to this uh it all depends on how  quickly  you are diagnosed from the point where  you show symptoms typically with  antivirals  especially for these k